WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.900
Not every document in a rack project has the same authority.

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.880
The base specification establishes shared interfaces within its scope.

3
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.180
An implementation specification describes a particular design, including choices that the base

4
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:12.740
leaves open.

5
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.100
A design guide helps equipment authors apply those interfaces, but its guidance

6
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.740
isn't automatically a new mandatory requirement.

7
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.120
The O R V three I T Gear Design Guide makes this

8
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.340
distinction explicitly: firm requirements reside in the base specification.

9
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.520
Read the relevant requirement and its applicability before treating advice as a

10
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.940
pass or fail criterion.

11
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.600
Likewise, don't transfer a dimension or payload from an implementation document to

12
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.140
the entire Open Rack family.

13
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.720
During a review, record the exact document, revision and supporting page.

14
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.460
Then connect the claim to the supplied product and its configuration.

15
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.240
A supplier can reasonably implement an allowed option without matching another operator's

16
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:52.660
design.

17
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.820
The task is to establish which interfaces must agree, which choices are

18
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.980
permitted, and whether the selected equipment is qualified together.

19
00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.780
This prevents both unnecessary rejection and unsupported compatibility claims.

